---
sitemap: false
---
{%- comment -%}
Hand-written sitemap. Deliberately NOT the jekyll-sitemap plugin, by decision.

The host comes from _data/mk.yml so there is ONE source of truth for it. Do not
hardcode a domain here. mk.yml said http when this file was written and the live
site enforced https, so the two disagreed; mk.yml was corrected and this file
followed it without being touched, which is the point. Keep it that way.

The extension test is load-bearing, and the way it is written is deliberate.
site.pages carries every file with front matter, which here includes
css/styles.css and the theme's assets/css/style.css — both were listed as
crawlable pages before this filter existed. The obvious test, page.output_ext,
does NOT work: Jekyll 3 exposes only content/dir/name/path/url to Liquid, so
page.output_ext is always nil and the filter silently drops EVERY page, leaving
a sitemap of posts only. Hence splitting page.name. Do not "simplify" it back.

Pages opt out with `sitemap: false` in their front matter. Unpublished posts
never reach site.posts, so drafts are excluded for free.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign base = site.data.mk.url -%}
<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<urlset xmlns="http://www.sitemaps.org/schemas/sitemap/0.9">
{%- for page in site.pages %}
{%- assign ext = page.name | split: '.' | last %}
{%- unless page.sitemap == false or ext != 'html' or page.url == '/404.html' %}
  <url>
    <loc>{{ base }}{{ page.url }}</loc>
  </url>
{%- endunless -%}
{%- endfor -%}
{%- for post in site.posts %}
  <url>
    <loc>{{ base }}{{ post.url }}</loc>
    <lastmod>{{ post.date | date: "%Y-%m-%d" }}</lastmod>
  </url>
{%- endfor %}
</urlset>
